1
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:06,630
So, I got the craziest e-mail this morning.

2
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:08,340
I don't mean to burst your bubble, dude,

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:11,250
but those penile enlargement pills do not work.

4
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:15,020
Believe me, I know.

5
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:19,550
The e-mail I got was from the office of Stephen Hawking.

6
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:20,790
- You're kidding. - Why?

7
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:23,100
He's coming to the university for a couple weeks to lecture,

8
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,540
and he's looking for an engineer to help maintain

9
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:27,260
the equipment on his wheelchair.

10
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,450
That's amazing.

11
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:30,280
You'll be like his pit crew.

12
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:34,820
A word of caution: I would not do

13
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:37,220
your Stephen Hawking impression in front of him.

14
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:40,420
You're right.

15
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:46,000
I suppose that could be considered offensive.

16
00:00:47,270 --> 00:00:49,530
Boy, Sheldon's going to freak out.

17
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:51,290
Yeah, he worships Hawking.

18
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:52,960
I was actually thinking about bringing him along

19
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,530
when I go over there so he can meet the great man.

20
00:00:55,530 --> 00:00:57,120
That's really nice of you, Howard.

21
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:58,400
It's no big deal.

22
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,930
Boy, a restraining order from Stephen Hawking.

23
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,900
It'll look so nice next to the ones he's already got

24
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:07,550
from Leonard Nimoy, Carl Sagan and Stan Lee.

25
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,360
Leonard, do you recall when I said

26
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:14,120
that I was going to revolutionize humanity's understanding

27
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,250
of the Higgs boson particle, and you said,

28
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:18,470
"Sheldon, it's 2:00 a.m., get out of my bedroom"?

29
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:21,270
Like it was ten hours ago.

30
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:22,180
What about it?

31
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:23,810
Well, I believe I've done it.

32
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,680
And I'm only saying "believe" to sound modest,

33
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:27,920
because, sweet Sam Houston, I did it.

34
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:30,520
- Really? - That's incredible. Oh, here.

35
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:31,690
Break out the math.

36
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:33,120
Oh, okay, let me see this.

37
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,450
All right, so this particle here

38
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:38,060
is the boson moving forward in time.

39
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:39,950
Now, I was thinking...

40
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,140
Howard, you go ahead and eat.

41
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:42,620
This isn't going to make any sense to you.

42
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,920
Sheldon, I have a working understanding of physics.

43
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:50,190
Yeah, good for you, and don't stop working on it.

44
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:53,700
Are you still going to tell him

45
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,750
- about you-know-who? - Yep.

46
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:56,980
Are you still going to introduce him?

47
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:58,510
Not on your life.

48
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,810
Please, please, please,

49
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:29,600
let me meet Hawking.

50
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:30,840
I told you, no.

51
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:32,310
But I said I'm sorry.

52
00:02:32,310 --> 00:02:35,870
No, you said, "Would it help if I said I'm sorry?"

53
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:38,580
And you never answered me.

54
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:40,740
So who owes whom an apology now?

55
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:43,620
Sheldon, you're a condescending jerk.

56
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:46,120
Why on earth would I want to do something nice for you?

57
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:48,660
To go to Jewish heaven?

58
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:52,450
Jews don't have heaven.

59
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:54,560
Then to avoid Jewish hell?

60
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:55,970
Have you met my mother?

61
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:57,410
I live in Jewish hell.

62
00:02:58,160 --> 00:02:59,600
Howard, please.

63
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:01,310
This is Stephen Hawking.

64
00:03:01,310 --> 00:03:03,640
Perhaps my only intellectual equal.

65
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:06,380
Oh, you can't be serious.

66
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:08,850
Try to put yourself in my place.

67
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:11,290
Imagine you're the sole human being

68
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:14,740
living on a planet populated with nothing but dogs.

69
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:16,290
And then it turns out

70
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:18,470
there's another human being.

71
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:22,380
Hang on.

72
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,630
Are you saying the rest of us are dogs?

73
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:28,170
Okay, I can see you're going to take this the wrong way.

74
00:03:28,170 --> 00:03:29,140
Let me try again.

75
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,850
Imagine you're the sole human being

76
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:35,160
living on a planet populated with nothing but chimps.

77
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:36,620
Get out of my lab.

78
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:38,980
Oh, now they're so much smarter than dogs.

79
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:40,900
Have you seen them on those little bicycles?

80
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:43,190
Get out.

81
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:45,700
How about dolphins?

82
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:46,750
Out!

83
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:51,840
So, come on, how was the first day with Hawking?

84
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:52,860
It was great.

85
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:54,330
We talked about movies...

86
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:56,190
Oh...

87
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,700
I showed him some card tricks...

88
00:03:57,700 --> 00:03:59,460
Oh!

89
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:01,900
He even let me read a couple pages from his new book.

90
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,680
Oh!

91
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,720
Something got you down there, bunky?

92
00:04:08,310 --> 00:04:11,480
Howard, please, I'm begging you.

93
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,180
Raj, you're our group historian.

94
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:14,880
Has Sheldon ever begged before?

95
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:16,190
Three times.

96
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,820
He begged the Fox network not to cancel Firefly.

97
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:24,600
He begged the TNT network to cancel Babylon 5.

98
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,630
And when he got food poisoning at the Rose Bowl Parade,

99
00:04:28,630 --> 00:04:30,370
he begged a deity he doesn't believe in

100
00:04:30,370 --> 00:04:31,980
to end his life quickly.

101
00:04:34,070 --> 00:04:36,670
Do you understand how important Hawking is to me?

102
00:04:36,670 --> 00:04:38,640
When I was six years old,

103
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,880
I dressed up as him for Halloween.

104
00:04:43,310 --> 00:04:44,210
You're kidding.

105
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:47,170
No, sir, no, I took my dad's desk chair,

106
00:04:47,170 --> 00:04:48,780
attached a Speak & Spell to it

107
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,540
and made my sister push me up and down the block

108
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:52,910
to trick or treat.

109
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:55,770
Granted most people thought I was R2-D2,

110
00:04:55,770 --> 00:04:58,100
but still, I got a lot of candy.

111
00:04:58,790 --> 00:05:00,630
You don't seem to be understanding

112
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:02,230
the English word "no."

113
00:05:02,230 --> 00:05:04,430
Maybe a different language will help.

114
00:05:04,430 --> 00:05:05,600
Russian: nyet.

115
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:07,260
Chinese: bu.

116
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:10,740
Klingon: qo'.

117
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:16,220
Binary coded Ascii:0110111001101111.

118
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,970
- It's actually 01100111. - No!

119
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:25,070
I'm not asking for me,

120
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:27,280
I'm asking for Hawking.

121
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:31,550
Let me try gangsta: Hells naw.

122
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,780
Okay, look, how about this?

123
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:40,380
Just give him my paper on the Higgs boson.

124
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:42,240
If he sees the incredible breakthrough I've made,

125
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:43,630
he'll reach out to me.

126
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:44,600
What if he doesn't?

127
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:46,500
He will; he's really smart.

128
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:50,760
That's an interesting idea.

129
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:52,380
Why don't you give me a minute

130
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:54,110
to talk it over with my friends?

131
00:05:54,110 --> 00:05:55,410
- How do I do that? - You walk away.

132
00:05:55,410 --> 00:05:56,400
Walking away.

133
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:08,990
You do realize you own his ass right now.

134
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:10,170
I do.

135
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:13,200
You can make him do anything you want.

136
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,680
Yeah, I know, I'm just trying to figure out

137
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:16,910
how much I want to punish him.

138
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,660
Well, don't be too mean.

139
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:19,480
Hey, fellas,

140
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:21,840
I'm thinking about making some freshly brewed iced tea

141
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:22,960
if anyone would like some.

142
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,180
I wouldn't mind a glass.

143
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:25,100
I wasn't talking to you.

144
00:06:28,350 --> 00:06:30,960
Bring him to his bony knees.

145
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:35,650
Sheldon, come on back.

146
00:06:35,650 --> 00:06:36,760
Yes, yes.

147
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:43,460
What did you decide?

148
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:46,530
I'll give your paper to Professor Hawking.

149
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:47,500
Great, thank you!

150
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:48,690
Oh, that's terrific!

151
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:53,870
But in exchange, I'd like you to do a few things for me.

152
00:06:53,870 --> 00:06:55,610
What kinds of things?

153
00:06:55,940 --> 00:07:00,200
Are you familiar with the 12 labors of Hercules?

154
00:07:02,050 --> 00:07:03,450
Of course.

155
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:05,790
You should be so lucky.

156
00:07:08,850 --> 00:07:10,720
All right.

157
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:12,360
What would you like me to do first?

158
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:14,330
Well, I thought I'd start you off

159
00:07:14,330 --> 00:07:16,360
by polishing my belt buckles.

160
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:18,550
By all means.

161
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:22,020
When I was a boy, I would polish my Mee-Maw's silver.

162
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,140
And she would entertain me with stories

163
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:25,740
about growing up in Oklahoma.

164
00:07:25,740 --> 00:07:27,040
Interesting woman.

165
00:07:27,040 --> 00:07:30,330
She once killed a prairie dog with a gravy boat.

166
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:33,420
That's nice.

167
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:45,880
That's a lot of belt buckles.

168
00:07:47,170 --> 00:07:49,200
Funny thing is,I only have one belt.

169
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:53,230
Anyway, I'll let you get started.

170
00:07:53,230 --> 00:07:54,240
Oh, by the way,

171
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:57,300
the little marks, uh,that look like water spots--

172
00:07:57,790 --> 00:08:00,660
I tend to stand too close to the urinal, so...

173
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:05,610
...what you're seeing there is splash back.

174
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:10,180
You make sissy on your belt buckles?

175
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:15,110
Mee-Maw's forks never had that.

176
00:08:16,410 --> 00:08:19,120
Here is a black light to check them.

177
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:20,380
And for your own peace of mind,

178
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:23,800
you might not want to shine that around the rest of the room.

179
00:08:33,070 --> 00:08:35,340
Sheldon, these look great.

180
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:38,040
They're like magnificent little crowns

181
00:08:38,040 --> 00:08:41,080
to hang over my magnificent little jewels.

182
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:44,600
How'd you get them so shiny?

183
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,470
Oh, I-I buffed them with Turtle Wax.

184
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:49,540
The man down at Pep Boys says from now on,

185
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:52,330
the urine should just bead up and roll right off.

186
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:54,750
Way to go the extra mile.

187
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:56,260
Your Mee-Maw would be proud.

188
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:58,230
My Mee-Maw must never know of this.

189
00:08:59,730 --> 00:09:02,160
Now will you give Professor Hawking my paper?

190
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:03,660
Oh, my dear boy, no.

191
00:09:03,660 --> 00:09:04,850
Okay.

192
00:09:05,580 --> 00:09:06,580
Next,

193
00:09:06,580 --> 00:09:10,140
this is a sexy French maid costume

194
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:11,400
I bought for Bernadette.

195
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:13,180
I thought it might spice things up

196
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:15,450
and get her to dust my room at the same time,

197
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:19,120
but I was wrong and really wrong.

198
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:21,780
And you want me to return it for you?

199
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:24,710
No, no, no, mon petit cherie.

200
00:09:39,830 --> 00:09:41,190
What are you all staring at?

201
00:09:41,750 --> 00:09:42,760
You ever seen a man try to get a meeting

202
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:44,890
with Stephen Hawking before?

203
00:09:58,330 --> 00:09:59,430
It's not Saturday night.

204
00:09:59,430 --> 00:10:01,320
Why are you doing your laundry?

205
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:04,160
This is not my laundry.

206
00:10:05,070 --> 00:10:06,910
Wow, are these Amy's?

207
00:10:06,910 --> 00:10:09,330
Kind of trashy -- good for her.

208
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,510
Those are Howard's.

209
00:10:14,490 --> 00:10:15,950
Why are you washing

210
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:18,410
Howard's man panties?

211
00:10:19,130 --> 00:10:20,460
Because if I don't,

212
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:23,320
he won't give my paper to Stephen Hawking.

213
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:25,060
He's a famous physicist.

214
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:27,950
I know, he's the wheelchair dude who invented time.

215
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:33,030
That's close enough.

216
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:34,610
I don't understand,

217
00:10:34,610 --> 00:10:36,900
why doesn't Howard just introduce you to the guy?

218
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:41,280
Because he's punishing me for being a "condescending jerk".

219
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:43,980
You don't think I'm condescending, do you?

220
00:10:44,180 --> 00:10:45,440
Well, kind of...

221
00:10:45,450 --> 00:10:47,710
Oh, I'm sorry,condescending means...

222
00:10:47,710 --> 00:10:49,750
I know what it means.

223
00:10:50,070 --> 00:10:51,740
And yes, you love correcting people

224
00:10:51,740 --> 00:10:52,950
and putting them down.

225
00:10:52,950 --> 00:10:54,190
Au contraire.

226
00:10:54,190 --> 00:10:57,320
When I correct people I am raising them up.

227
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:01,650
You should know, I do it for you more than anyone.

228
00:11:04,170 --> 00:11:06,130
Come on, you do it to feel superior.

229
00:11:06,130 --> 00:11:07,330
I see that twinkle in your eye

230
00:11:07,330 --> 00:11:09,600
when someone says "who" instead of "whom"

231
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,920
or thinks the moon is a planet.

232
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:16,910
Or Don Quixote is a book about a donkey named Hotay.

233
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:19,010
See, there it is,

234
00:11:19,010 --> 00:11:20,660
there's that twinkle.

235
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:23,220
Well, I can't help it. That's an involuntary twinkle.

236
00:11:24,630 --> 00:11:26,870
What do you want me to tell you, Sheldon?

237
00:11:26,870 --> 00:11:28,010
I want you to tell me

238
00:11:28,010 --> 00:11:30,490
that Howard is being mean to me for no reason.

239
00:11:30,490 --> 00:11:33,890
Fine, Howard is being mean to you for no reason.

240
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:35,490
I knew it.

241
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:42,000
What's the matter?

242
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,200
Every time I spend the night,

243
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,910
your mom slaps me on the behind says, "Go get 'em."

244
00:11:48,730 --> 00:11:49,780
It's not her fault.

245
00:11:49,780 --> 00:11:52,450
She's getting hormone replacement therapy.

246
00:11:54,950 --> 00:11:56,980
Makes her crazy horny.

247
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:02,470
Check this out, I got Sheldon to wear the Frenchmaid's costume.

248
00:12:02,510 --> 00:12:04,510
Oh, my God, that's terrible.

249
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:06,890
Hey, I gave you first crack at it.

250
00:12:08,290 --> 00:12:09,360
Why are you doing that?

251
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:10,740
You're being mean to him.

252
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:12,330
He's mean to me all the time.

253
00:12:12,330 --> 00:12:14,970
You've heard him tease me about not having a doctorate.

254
00:12:14,990 --> 00:12:16,410
If you don't want to get teased about that,

255
00:12:16,410 --> 00:12:17,890
get a doctorate.

256
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:22,270
I have one; they're great.

257
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:26,810
Oh, come on, the man torments me.

258
00:12:26,810 --> 00:12:29,520
I'm just letting him have a little taste of his own medicine.

259
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:31,120
It's not the same thing.

260
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:32,720
Sheldon doesn't know when he's being mean

261
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:34,620
because the part of his brain that should know

262
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:37,370
is getting a wedgie from the rest of his brain.

263
00:12:40,190 --> 00:12:42,540
Fine, I'll just make him do a couple more things,

264
00:12:42,540 --> 00:12:43,680
then I'll stop.

265
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:45,620
No, you have to stop now.

266
00:12:45,620 --> 00:12:48,580
Bernadette, I want to remind you,

267
00:12:48,580 --> 00:12:52,440
you promised to take me dress shopping tomorrow!

268
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:54,290
Oh, damn.

269
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:58,890
I'm sorry, I can't make it,

270
00:12:59,210 --> 00:13:02,330
but Sheldon's going to go with you!

271
00:13:09,310 --> 00:13:12,030
And that's the last thing you do to him.

272
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:18,010
Sheldon!

273
00:13:19,430 --> 00:13:21,870
I need your help!

274
00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:25,390
What is it now, Mrs. Wolowitz?

275
00:13:25,390 --> 00:13:26,870
It's this dress!

276
00:13:26,870 --> 00:13:30,680
When I put my front in, my back pops out!

277
00:13:33,590 --> 00:13:37,510
When I put my back in,my front pops out!

278
00:13:38,690 --> 00:13:42,490
It's like trying to keep two dogs in a bathtub!

279
00:13:44,370 --> 00:13:46,190
What do you want me to do?

280
00:13:46,450 --> 00:13:48,750
We're gonna have to work as a team!

281
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:52,640
Get in here, grab a handful,and start stuffing!

282
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,820
I'm not sure how to do this.

283
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:07,180
It's easy, just pretend you're putting away a sleeping bag.

284
00:14:08,950 --> 00:14:12,470
Sleeping bags don't usually sweat this much, but okay.

285
00:14:14,260 --> 00:14:15,780
Now zip me up.

286
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:17,460
Oh, if we squeeze you any tighter,

287
00:14:17,460 --> 00:14:19,340
you may turn into a diamond.

288
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:25,710
You're right, who am I kidding?

289
00:14:25,710 --> 00:14:28,940
You should have seen me when I was young, Sheldon.

290
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:31,100
The fellas used to line up

291
00:14:31,100 --> 00:14:33,640
and bring me boxes of candy.

292
00:14:34,670 --> 00:14:36,910
Why did I eat it all?!

293
00:14:40,350 --> 00:14:42,010
Would you hold me?

294
00:14:42,020 --> 00:14:45,350
Oh... no, you know, I'm not really the holding kind of...

295
00:14:50,630 --> 00:14:52,190
Oh, guys, I just remembered.

296
00:14:52,190 --> 00:14:55,590
I got your ome Hawking souvenirs.

297
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:56,860
What are these?

298
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:58,960
Gears and springs from his wheelchair.

299
00:14:58,970 --> 00:15:00,230
Pretty cool, huh?

300
00:15:01,970 --> 00:15:03,850
Wow, that's amazing.

301
00:15:03,850 --> 00:15:06,270
I made an adjustment on the motor drive

302
00:15:06,270 --> 00:15:08,470
and when I was putting it back together

303
00:15:08,470 --> 00:15:11,590
I could not for the life of me figure out where they went.

304
00:15:15,190 --> 00:15:16,310
I did it.

305
00:15:16,310 --> 00:15:18,620
Had to go to three clothing stores,

306
00:15:18,620 --> 00:15:22,470
but we finally found a dress that could envelop your mother.

307
00:15:24,580 --> 00:15:25,420
I should have sent you

308
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:28,060
to the custom car cover place in Altadena.

309
00:15:28,060 --> 00:15:30,170
They have her pattern on file.

310
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:32,650
Humorous.

311
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:37,540
Now will you please present my paper to Professor Hawking?

312
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:38,560
I don't know.

313
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:39,940
Oh, for heaven's sake.

314
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,630
I did your laundry, I pee-pee-proofed your belt buckles,

315
00:15:43,630 --> 00:15:45,460
I, I even sprained my wrist

316
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:47,440
helping your mother lift her bosom.

317
00:15:52,810 --> 00:15:53,850
All right, Sheldon,

318
00:15:53,850 --> 00:15:56,390
there's only one thing left I want you to do.

319
00:15:56,390 --> 00:15:58,090
Don't worry, it's an easy one.

320
00:15:58,090 --> 00:15:59,460
Okay.

321
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:01,630
Give me a compliment.

322
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:03,590
Fine.

323
00:16:03,590 --> 00:16:05,560
You have very tiny hands.

324
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:11,330
No... about my job.

325
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:14,930
I want you to tell me I'm good at what I do.

326
00:16:15,210 --> 00:16:17,420
You're obviously good at what you do.

327
00:16:17,420 --> 00:16:20,380
Well, then why are you always ripping on me?

328
00:16:20,380 --> 00:16:22,550
Oh, I understand the confusion.

329
00:16:22,550 --> 00:16:25,650
I have never said that you are not good at what you do.

330
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:29,390
It's just that what you do is not worth doing.

331
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:37,060
It's nicer than anything he's ever said to me.

332
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:38,500
I'd take it and run.

333
00:16:40,630 --> 00:16:42,160
Thank you, Sheldon.

334
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:44,320
Now will you give my paper to Hawking?

335
00:16:44,320 --> 00:16:45,570
Sorry, I can't.

336
00:16:45,570 --> 00:16:46,640
What, why not?

337
00:16:46,640 --> 00:16:48,840
I gave it to him three days ago.

338
00:16:49,540 --> 00:16:51,140
He was really impressed.

339
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:52,820
He wants to meet you.

340
00:16:54,510 --> 00:16:56,140
All right, then.

341
00:16:57,840 --> 00:16:59,250
Thank you, Howard.

342
00:17:00,050 --> 00:17:01,460
Please let Professor Hawking know

343
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:04,290
that I'm available at his earliest convenience.

344
00:17:06,450 --> 00:17:08,160
I thought he might be a little more excited.

345
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:09,160
Give it a second.

346
00:17:18,210 --> 00:17:20,470
Professor Hawking,

347
00:17:20,470 --> 00:17:24,000
it's an honor and a privilege to meet you, sir.

348
00:17:26,750 --> 00:17:28,180
I know.

349
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:33,040
I want to thank you for taking time to see me.

350
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:35,800
My pleasure.

351
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:38,920
I enjoyed reading your paper very much.

352
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:41,810
You clearly have a brilliant mind.

353
00:17:41,810 --> 00:17:43,150
I know.

354
00:17:45,860 --> 00:17:49,250
Your thesis that the Higgs boson is a black hole

355
00:17:49,250 --> 00:17:53,570
accelerating backwards through time is fascinating.

356
00:17:53,570 --> 00:17:55,070
Thank you.

357
00:17:55,070 --> 00:17:58,560
It just-- it came to me one morning in the shower.

358
00:18:01,560 --> 00:18:03,220
That's nice.

359
00:18:03,220 --> 00:18:05,380
Too bad it's wrong.

360
00:18:08,530 --> 00:18:10,720
What do you mean wrong?

361
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:15,820
You made an arithmetic mistake on page two.

362
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:20,000
It was quite the boner.

363
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:26,430
No, no... that can't be right.

364
00:18:26,430 --> 00:18:28,790
I-I don't make arithmetic mistakes.

365
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,790
Are you saying I do?

366
00:18:31,790 --> 00:18:32,720
Oh, no,

367
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:34,150
no, no, of course not.

368
00:18:34,150 --> 00:18:35,510
It's just, I was thinking...

369
00:18:35,510 --> 00:18:38,270
Oh, gosh, golly,I made a boo-boo,

370
00:18:38,270 --> 00:18:40,960
and I gave it to Stephen Hawking.

371
00:18:46,450 --> 00:18:50,340
Great, another fainter.

